// claude-settings.json — THE STACK'S OWN CLAUDE SETTINGS.
//
// Copy this next to `mounts` in the stack dir and let Ansible template it (see the claude_box role's
// claude_box_config_files). On every box spawn the broker DEEP-MERGES it into the shared
// ~/.claude/settings.json — the file every box and the hub see at ~/.claude, which also holds the
// login and which claude rewrites by itself. That is why this file exists at all: the settings you
// want are project policy, but their home is a file no deploy may overwrite.
//
// What that means in practice:
//   * only the keys named here are touched — everything else in settings.json is left alone;
//   * objects merge key by key, so `permissions.defaultMode` does not take the whole `permissions`
//     block with it. Lists REPLACE: a list is the value you asked for;
//   * DELETE a key here and the next spawn takes it back out of settings.json — unless someone has
//     changed that value in the meantime, which makes it theirs, not ours;
//   * whole-line `//` comments (like these) are stripped before parsing. Nothing else about the file
//     is special: it is JSON;
//   * a syntax error means the settings are not applied, loudly, in the broker's log — never a box
//     that will not spawn. `docker compose logs box-broker | grep claude-settings` after a deploy.
//
// The muster activity hooks are re-applied AFTER this file, so a `hooks` block here cannot switch
// off the hub's view of what a box is doing.
{
  // A status line for every box's claude. The script has to exist inside the box: put it in the
  // shared claude home (data/claude/, which is ~/.claude everywhere) and Ansible can sync it there
  // without going anywhere near settings.json.
  "statusLine": {
    "type": "command",
    "command": "bash ~/.claude/statusline-command.sh"
  }

  // Anything else claude takes in settings.json can go here too, e.g.:
  // "model": "opus",
  // "permissions": { "defaultMode": "acceptEdits" }
}
